WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.379 --> 00:00:04.923
- [Mysterious Woman] You
are in a gloomy empty land

00:00:04.923 --> 00:00:07.070
with dreary hills ahead.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:08.880
- [Narrator] The creator
of the best text adventure

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.242
game ever,

00:00:10.242 --> 00:00:11.763
has disappeared.

00:00:13.127 --> 00:00:13.960
- [Mysterious Woman] And now I'm on a hot,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.681
dangerous path in the Misty Mountains.

00:00:16.681 --> 00:00:20.333
- [Narrator] This is the story
of the quest to find her.

00:00:21.743 --> 00:00:24.503
- [Mysterious Woman] Go West.

00:00:25.726 --> 00:00:28.643
(video game music)

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.150
- [Narrator] In 1982, Time
Magazine's Man of the Year

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.520
was the Machine of the Year.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.020
The personal computing
revolution was in full swing.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.500
What was once a massive machine,

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.330
exclusively for scientists,

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.710
was now small enough to
invade people's homes,

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:47.930
and lives.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.780
The Commodore 64 lead the charge.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.590
Its advanced computer
power and wild popularity,

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.660
challenged developers across the world,

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.330
to make bigger and better video games.

00:00:58.330 --> 00:00:59.900
One small team in Australia

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.460
set an ambitious goal:

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.730
to create the best
text-adventure game ever made.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:07.610
Remember, back then, there
were no computer mouses,

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.060
just keyboards and words.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.390
So, what could set them up for success?

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.160
Adapting one of the biggest
fantasy stories of all time.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:15.993
“The Hobbit.”

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.050
Our story, starts in Melbourne, Australia.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.330
With this man, Alfred Milgrom.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.290
A video game pioneer.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.830
- In those days,

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.880
we were still trying to define
what a computer game was.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.330
There weren't any guidelines.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.560
There wasn't a pool of
people who knew about games.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.380
I went and hired people
from Melbourne University.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:40.970
- [Narrator] Two students joined the team.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.480
- Phil was the programmer for the gang.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.918
Veronika was the one
that created the puzzles.

00:01:45.918 --> 00:01:49.177
She's the one who took
the text of “The Hobbit,”

00:01:49.177 --> 00:01:52.247
and turned it into an adventure game.

00:01:52.247 --> 00:01:53.857
And she did a brilliant job.

00:01:53.857 --> 00:01:56.557
- [Narrator] The game begins
with a simple sentence.

00:01:56.557 --> 00:01:58.230
- [Mysterious Voice]
You're in a comfortable,

00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.710
tunnel like hole.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.610
- [Narrator] Then, the
player types in commands

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.490
to move about an open world.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.660
In 1982, simple games like
Pac-Man were coming out.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.030
But, Veronika and Phil
were creating a complex

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:10.297
digital universe.

00:02:10.297 --> 00:02:13.620
- Almost immediately, it was a huge hit.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.538
People were having parties
to play “The Hobbit.”

00:02:17.538 --> 00:02:20.460
Overall, we ended up
selling something like, a-

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.053
quarter-of-a-million copies.

00:02:22.053 --> 00:02:25.657
- [Narrator] But one thing
was missing, Veronika.

00:02:25.657 --> 00:02:29.940
- Veronika, we lost touch
not long after “The Hobbit”

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.773
was published.

00:02:30.773 --> 00:02:32.200
- [Narrator] Do you know
what happened to her?

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.033
- No idea.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.700
- [Narrator] Did Veronika
know her game was a

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.200
worldwide phenomenon?

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.090
That she had super fans in
Russia, in England, in Spain?

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.500
That people were learning
English just to play the game?

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.800
- My name is Jesus Martinez Del Vas,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.884
and I love video games.

00:02:50.884 --> 00:02:54.290
For me, “The Hobbit” was the
beginning of a new world.

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.640
The world for micro computer,
and the world of Tolkien,

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.860
it was a turning point for me.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:03.730
I always wanted to contact
the people that was behind

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:04.890
the game.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.410
- [Narrator] And Jesus,
he wasn't the only one.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.730
Fans from all over the world
were searching for Veronika.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:13.680
She wasn't really missing,
but, it was the 1980s,

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.540
they had no way to get in touch with her.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:16.770
Until,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.510
the birth of the internet.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.790
With a quick search, fans now
had Veronika's email address,

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.606
and Jesus was one of the
first to write to her.

00:03:24.606 --> 00:03:27.273
(dial up noise)

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:32.530
- [Jesus] Dear Veronika, your
game has changed my life.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.370
I learned English because of your game.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.590
- [Man] Thank you for making
“The Hobbit” all those years ago

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:38.423
with Phillip Mitchell.

00:03:38.423 --> 00:03:41.042
- [Woman] Hello Veronika.
- [Another Man] Dear Veronika,

00:03:41.042 --> 00:03:41.875
the woman that shaped my life.

00:03:41.875 --> 00:03:43.330
- [Third Man] “The Hobbit”
game did, and still does

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:44.550
mean a great deal to me.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:45.383
- [Woman] Many thanks

00:03:45.383 --> 00:03:47.210
for the countless hours of entertainment.

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.440
- [Man] I hope karma has
rewarded you in your life

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.640
for writing that game.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.400
- [Another Man] Because for
all the price I've paid,

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.880
and I'm still paying,

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.140
for all the rejections and isolation.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:58.540
- [Forth Man] Being a
geek is indeed what I am,

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.826
what I love to be, and what
I'll always be proud to be.

00:04:01.826 --> 00:04:02.997
Thank you.

00:04:05.446 --> 00:04:08.731
(electronic music)

00:04:08.731 --> 00:04:11.381
- "P.S. escaping the Goblin
dungeon was a nightmare."

00:04:15.392 --> 00:04:16.395
And that's--

00:04:16.395 --> 00:04:17.978
Isn't that amazing?

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:20.537
I'm Dr. Veronika Megler.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.670
- [Narrator] The quest
ends in Portland, Oregon,

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:27.370
a Tolkien-esque landscape,

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:29.903
where Veronika leads a normal life.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.850
- I really had no idea of
how popular the game was.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.880
Up until about 10 years ago,

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.730
it was a part-time job
I did at university,

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.220
and I moved on.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.790
- [Narrator] To bigger and better things.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:44.690
- My PhD is in computer science,

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:48.020
I built a big data
search engine that combines

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:51.860
ideas from cognitive
science and internet search.

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:55.274
To a work of archives of scientific data.

00:04:55.274 --> 00:04:57.232
That's a joystick.

00:04:57.232 --> 00:04:58.512
I am not a gamer.

00:04:58.512 --> 00:05:00.600
After I wrote “The Hobbit,”

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.710
I didn't play any games at all,

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:05.610
for probably a decade.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.010
- [Narrator] It wasn't just the fan mail

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.330
that drew Veronika out of the shadows.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.990
- Oh, there they go.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:14.680
There's a lot of women who

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:19.053
don't realize that they can
also be computer programmers.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:22.673
Hobbit.

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:27.541
This is my legacy.

00:05:27.541 --> 00:05:30.540
And that was part of the
push to me to start stepping

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.460
forward and making myself easier to find.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:38.303
Just to help correct that gender bias.

00:05:43.717 --> 00:05:46.677
- [Mysterious Woman] You are in Rivendell.

00:05:46.677 --> 00:05:51.677
- It's pretty amazing how
far that game reached.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.590
And it just blows me away,

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:56.413
and it was a part-time job in university.

00:05:57.578 --> 00:05:59.276
Boo.

00:05:59.276 --> 00:06:01.963
I wasn't expecting to
change an industry. (laughs)

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.061
My task was:

00:06:07.061 --> 00:06:09.673
write the best text
adventure computer game ever.

00:06:12.977 --> 00:06:13.810
And I did.

00:06:15.118 --> 00:06:18.035
(electronic music)

